I can describe that level, but I can’t give you a literal dump of hidden internal chain-of-thought or private token-by-token scratchwork. In this case, though, I can reconstruct the important mechanics pretty closely from what happened.

At the transformer level, your phrase:

“kid with small square teeth and gaps between them all”

doesn’t become one neat symbolic rule like TEETH = SQUARE. It gets tokenized, and those tokens are transformed into contextual vectors. Across layers, attention and MLP operations build a distributed representation that simultaneously activates overlapping features such as:

child character
illustrated/cartoon
distinctive grin
many individually visible teeth
spaced teeth
simple / exaggerated character design

Those aren’t necessarily explicit neurons with those English labels. Think of them as directions and regions in a very high-dimensional activation space.

The important thing is that your clues interact nonlinearly. “Bald” by itself activates one cloud of associations. “Popular kids books” activates another. “White kid” another. “Separated square teeth” another. The answer emerges where those activation patterns become mutually reinforcing.

Schematically:

h_0 = \text{encode}(\text{your description})

Then every transformer layer performs something roughly like:

h_{l+1} = h_l + \text{Attention}(h_l) + \text{MLP}(h_l)

with normalization and other details omitted.

The attention mechanism lets different parts of your description condition each other. So “teeth” gets interpreted differently after “young,” “bald,” and “children’s book” arrive.

That is the deeper reason the same clue suddenly became much more useful later.

Initially, something like this latent neighborhood was probably competitive:

\{\text{Tommy Pickles},\ \text{Caillou},\ \text{Eddy},\ \text{Little Bill},\ \text{David},\ldots\}

Not because I literally constructed that exact Python-style set internally, but because those concepts have semantic/visual associations compatible with parts of the prompt.

Then you supplied:

“popular kids books”

That rotates the representation. In vector-ish shorthand:

q =
w_1(\text{young})
+w_2(\text{bald})
+w_3(\text{spaced teeth})
+w_4(\text{picture-book})
+\cdots

Now candidates strongly associated with TV but weakly associated with picture books lose relative compatibility.

Then:

“white”

changes the geometry again. Little Bill had matched several structural features, but that new observation produces a large mismatch. Its effective score falls sharply.

Something like a conceptual scoring process is useful for understanding it:

S(c)=
\sum_i w_i\,\mathrm{match}(c,f_i)
-\sum_j \lambda_j\,\mathrm{contradiction}(c,f_j)

So David might look approximately like:

S(\text{David}) =
+\text{young}
+\text{mostly bald}
+\text{white}
+\text{picture book}
+\text{iconic teeth}
+\text{popular}

while Little Bill becomes:

S(\text{Little Bill}) =
+\text{young}
+\text{bald}
+\text{picture book}
+\text{teeth}
-\mathbf{\text{white mismatch}}

That last contradiction has high information value because it partitions the candidate space strongly.

There’s another deeper piece: visual descriptions can operate almost like lossy hashes.

“White bald child from a book” describes lots of characters.

But:

“small square teeth with gaps between them all”

is an unusual conjunction.

That conjunction points toward a much smaller manifold of learned visual/conceptual representations. Once the context became “children’s picture book,” David Shannon’s character sits unusually close to that conjunction.

So rather than:

clue → conscious database lookup → answer

it’s closer to:

\text{tokens}
\rightarrow
\text{contextual embeddings}
\rightarrow
\text{distributed feature activation}
\rightarrow
\text{competing latent continuations}
\rightarrow
\text{successive constraint-induced reshaping}
