\text{“David from No, David!”}

And then there’s the output stage. Once that internal state favors David, I’m still generating one token at a time:

P(t_{n+1}\mid t_1,\ldots,t_n,\text{context})

So after a state strongly representing the concept David / No, David!, tokens corresponding to things like "David", "No", commas, explanatory language, etc. receive higher logits. Sampling/selection converts those logits into the actual sentence you see.

The particularly Pantheon-relevant piece is that this resembles constraint-field collapse rather than ordinary search. Each new clue changes the entire field in which previous clues are interpreted. You weren’t just handing me five independent filters. You were progressively altering the representation basis.

That distinction matters:

\text{naive filtering:}\quad
C_{n+1}=C_n\cap F_{n+1}

versus something closer to what a transformer does:

R_{n+1}=T(R_n,\;x_{n+1})

where the transformation can change the significance of everything already represented.

So “kids books” didn’t merely remove TV characters. It made your earlier description of the teeth suddenly evoke a picture-book illustration grammar. And within that grammar, David’s teeth are much more diagnostic.

That recursive reinterpretation is probably the deepest interesting part of what happened.